<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pērkonkrusts</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1933">1933</a>–<a href="/years/1934">1934</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Thunder Cross</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pērkonkrusts (Thunder Cross) was a Latvian ultranationalist, antisemitic and anti-German organisation founded in 1933 by Gustavs Celmiņš. It was banned in 1934, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlis-ulmanis">Kārlis Ulmanis</a>'s coup, and its leaders were imprisoned. During the German occupation some former members took part in the persecution and murder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, notably in the Arājs Kommando.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/perkonkrusts.jpg" alt="Pērkonkrusts" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: RootOfAllLight · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P%C4%93rkonkrusts_logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Celmiņš had founded the fascist group Ugunskrusts (Fire Cross) in 1932, and when the government outlawed it the organisation reappeared at once under the name Pērkonkrusts. Thunder Cross is one of the Latvian names for the swastika, which served as its symbol; members wore a grey shirt and black beret and greeted one another with a variant of the Roman salute and the words Cīņai sveiks. By 1934 it is estimated to have had between 5,000 and 6,000 members, drawn mostly from towns such as Riga, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesis">Cēsis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valmiera">Valmiera</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jelgava">Jelgava</a> and from students at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Its slogan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Latvians</a>! Work and bread for Latvians!, summed up its aim of placing all political and economic control in the hands of ethnic Latvians. It rejected the laws that gave national minorities cultural autonomy, directed its propaganda against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-germans">Baltic Germans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, whom it accused of taking over the economy, and attacked parliamentary politicians as corrupt. It rejected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> as foreign and proposed Dievturība, an attempted revival of pre-Christian Latvian religion. The historian Roger Griffin called it a small but genuine fascist opposition, while Uldis Krēsliņš has argued that it was neither affiliated with nor a copy of German Nazism.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlis-ulmanis">Kārlis Ulmanis</a>'s coup of May 1934, which banned all parties and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saeima">Saeima</a>, Celmiņš was imprisoned for three years and then banished, setting up a liaison office in Helsinki. Under the Soviet occupation of 1940 to 1941 more members were arrested and some deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>. When the Germans invaded in June 1941 the group was briefly allowed to operate, and former members were recruited for the Arajs Commando; historians Rudīte Vīksne and Andrievs Ezergailis hold that only a handful took part in the killing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, their role lying more in antisemitic propaganda. The Germans banned it in August 1941.</p><p>From February 1942 Celmiņš headed the committee recruiting Latvian men for the auxiliary police battalions, which took part in massacres of rural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and other civilians. His underground anti-German paper Brīvā Latvija led to his arrest and imprisonment in Flossenbürg concentration camp. A new group took the name in 1995; two of its members were killed on 5 June 1997 while trying to bomb a Soviet war monument in Riga.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pērkonkrusts” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 15 May 1934 Prime Minister Kārlis Ulmanis, backed by the army and the Aizsargi, suspended the Saeima and banned political parties, including the radical right <b>Pērkonkrusts</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Republic of Latvia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
